R/foundation_embed_coords.R. Each of the five source fetches swallows its error into `NULL` with a `message()` (not a `warning()`), so nothing is signalled to the caller: lines 264-269 (SoilGrids loop over 5 vars), 281-285 (WorldClim prec, 12 layers), 297-301 (WorldClim tavg, 12 layers), 313-317 (bio fallback), 329-339 (SRTM elev, with a nested fallback). Each is followed by `if (!is.null(x)) { layers$... <- ... }`. The ONLY completeness check is at lines 359-361:
```r
if (length(layers) == 0L)
  stop("No layers could be built. Check network + geodata cache.", call. = FALSE)
```
There is no assertion that `schema == "v1_encoder"` produced 31 layers. The result is then written to the schema-keyed cache path at line 366 (`terra::writeRaster(stk, stack_path, overwrite = TRUE)`), and lines 243-247 short-circuit every later call:
```r
if (!force && file.exists(stack_path)) {
  message(sprintf("  Using cached stack: %s", stack_path))
  return(terra::rast(stack_path))
}
```
The documented contract is explicit about the count -- R/foundation_embed_coords.R:186-198 promises exactly "5 SoilGrids + 12 monthly prec + 12 monthly tavg + 2 SRTM" = 31 layers, matched to the 31-channel `edaphos-cerrado-moco-v1` encoder (R/foundation_weights.R:57).

The two loops use different index bases but the identical guard. R, `for (iter in seq_len(nmcmc))` so iter runs 1..nmcmc -- R/pilar7_bayesian_hierarchical.R:314:
```r
if (iter > burn) w_mean <- w_mean + w_cur / (nmcmc - burn)
```
accumulates iters burn+1..nmcmc = exactly `nmcmc - burn` terms. Correct.
C++, `for (int iter = 0; iter < nmcmc; ++iter)` so iter runs 0..nmcmc-1 -- src/bhs_gibbs_rcpp.cpp:132-134:
```cpp
if (iter > burn) {
    w_mean += w_cur / static_cast<double>(nmcmc - burn);
}
```
accumulates iters burn+1..nmcmc-1 = only `nmcmc - burn - 1` terms, still divided by `nmcmc - burn`. (The thinning index immediately below, at :140-143, correctly starts at `it = burn`, confirming the intended 0-based post-burn start is `burn`, not `burn+1`.)
The file header asserts equivalence: src/bhs_gibbs_rcpp.cpp:15-16 -- "so the posterior chain is statistically equivalent up to RNG-draw order". Nothing tests it: `grep w_post_mean tests/testthat/*.R` returns no hits, and tests/testthat/test-pilar7-bhs-rcpp.R:38 compares only beta posterior means "within 0.2".

Two gaps, both reachable from documented arguments of the exported `quantum_kernel()`.
(a) Memory. R-side validation (R/quantum_kernel.R:167-181) warns only for the non-default backend: `if (n > 12L && backend == "r")`. The default is `backend = "rcpp"` (R/quantum_kernel.R:163), which reaches src/quantum_kernel_rcpp.cpp:133-136:
```cpp
if (n > 20) {
    Rcpp::warning("quantum_kernel_rcpp with n_qubits > 20 may exhaust RAM.");
}
const size_t dim = (size_t) 1 << n;
```
A warning, then it proceeds to allocate `nX` vectors of `dim` `std::complex<double>` (:138). At n=25 that is 16 bytes * 2^25 = 537 MB *per sample*; at n=30, 17 GB per sample. For n >= 64, `(size_t)1 << n` is undefined behaviour. The path is user-reachable without touching the C++: R/quantum_foundation.R:53-55 validates `n_pcs` with only `n_pcs >= 1L`, so `qf_krr_fit(embeddings, y, n_pcs = 26L)` -> `quantum_kernel` -> 26 qubits.
(b) reps. Documented as `>= 1` at R/quantum_kernel.R:109-110 but never enforced. src/quantum_kernel_rcpp.cpp:95 is `for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r)`, so `reps <= 0` applies no gates: `zz_feature_map` leaves the state at |0...0> (set at :92-93), every overlap is 1, and the function returns an all-ones matrix. The R backend diverges -- R/quantum_kernel.R:76 is `for (r in seq_len(as.integer(reps)))`, and `seq_len(-1L)` errors ("argument must be coercible to non-negative integer") while `seq_len(0L)` returns `integer(0)`, so `reps = -1` errors under `backend="r"` and silently returns ones under `backend="rcpp"`.

R/active_batchbald.R:67-77:
```r
.batchbald_aleatoric_var <- function(al_model) {
  rg <- al_model$model
  oob <- tryCatch(stats::na.omit(rg$predictions), error = function(e) NULL)
  y_train <- al_model$labeled[[al_model$target]]
  if (!is.null(oob) && length(oob) == length(y_train)) {
    stats::var(y_train - oob)
  } else {
    in_sample <- stats::predict(rg, data = al_model$labeled)$predictions
    stats::var(y_train - in_sample)
  }
}
```
`na.omit()` shortens the vector, so the `length(oob) == length(y_train)` test fails whenever even one observation was never out-of-bag -- precisely the small-n / small-num.trees regime active learning operates in, and the same regime that triggers the ranger "Too few trees" error documented in another finding. The fallback is silent: no warning distinguishes the two branches. Verified with ranger 0.18.0 that in the normal case `rg$predictions` is a length-n numeric with 0 NAs, so the OOB branch is the intended one.
